WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.040
What was that? A face? Just as quickly as I&nbsp;
had glimpsed it, it was gone. It was cold as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.560
death and twice as dark in here. Seriously,&nbsp;
I’d never been anywhere so dark. It was more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.920
than just the absence of light, it was like&nbsp;
walking through thick, black water, only able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:21.600
to see inches in front of my face. Things moved&nbsp;
slower than they should have. Every step I took,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.040
further down towards some unknowable&nbsp;
destination, the darker and colder it got.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.440
I found myself wondering, “Is this&nbsp;
what dying feels like? Slipping down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.680
further into nothingness, step by step,&nbsp;
through darkness that never ends?”

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:41.760
It was a stairway. Like Jacob’s Ladder, except&nbsp;
instead of up to heaven, it was a one-way trip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:47.120
straight down. There was some distant, tinny&nbsp;
whining noise that I couldn’t quite make out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.840
the way things sound when you’re drowning.&nbsp;
I didn’t know what possibility was scarier:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.880
That the steps just went on forever, and I’d&nbsp;
be walking until my bones turned to dust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.560
or that something would be at the&nbsp;
bottom, waiting for me in the dark.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:06.080
The deeper I got, the more I got this sense that&nbsp;
the latter was true. The way you can feel yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:11.600
being watched, or sense someone standing behind&nbsp;
you. That evolutionary holdover from when humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:16.800
still took part in the world of predators and&nbsp;
prey, and we were not the predators. Some part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:22.640
of us is just built to know when true danger is&nbsp;
lurking nearby, or down below, patient and hungry.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.520
I could feel its malice rising up the stairs&nbsp;
towards me - The way people in a skyscraper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.160
catching fire can feel the heat coming up from&nbsp;
below, and know it’s already too late for them.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:38.240
For the first time, my eyes came into focus,&nbsp;
and I made something out in the distance. When I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.440
realized what it was - and worse, how strikingly&nbsp;
familiar it was - all I could do was scream.&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.000
That’s when I woke up, drenched in sweat,&nbsp;
and relieved to see the darkness of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.480
stairway replaced by bright morning light&nbsp;
shining in through my bedroom window.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.920
My brother, Steve, was standing there&nbsp;
with a concerned look on his face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.880
He told me I’d been screaming in my sleep, and&nbsp;
asked me if I’d been having “That Dream” again.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:08.000
It made me feel like an idiot, but I had to nod&nbsp;
and tell him, yes, it was the same one. This weird&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.200
nightmare I’d been having day after day, and&nbsp;
despite it almost always being the same thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:17.920
it somehow never got any less frightening.&nbsp;
There was no way to even describe the profound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.320
sense of dread I felt walking down those&nbsp;
stairs, and finally seeing what was waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.600
for me below - Even though I could never&nbsp;
actually remember what it was upon waking.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:32.400
Steve told me not to worry about it and tossed&nbsp;
some clothes onto my bed. He assured me that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:37.040
was probably just finals stress, seeing as&nbsp;
we were a mere few months from graduating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.200
I needed to be up and at ‘em today: We were&nbsp;
touring one of our first-choice colleges.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:45.600
My brother and I had always been close.&nbsp;
Even if we hadn’t been fraternal twins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.160
I think we still would have been best friends.&nbsp;
All through elementary, middle, and high school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.320
we were practically joined at the hip, so it&nbsp;
only made sense that when we were getting close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.720
to graduation, we wanted to go to the same&nbsp;
college. We’d be friends to the end, till&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.800
we were old men on the porch in rocking chairs.
It so happened that we were together in the end…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.080
just not in the way I’d hoped.
The two of us arrived to check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.400
out the potential college a few hours later.&nbsp;
It was a nice day out; the brightness of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.760
sky and the lush greenery across campus quickly&nbsp;
banished the bitter memories of the bad dream.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:23.440
High school hadn’t exactly been a picnic for me&nbsp;
- I’m the nervous type if you couldn’t already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:28.400
tell. Steve, who was the more outgoing and&nbsp;
confident one, always looked out for me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:33.280
He’d always been there whenever I was dealing&nbsp;
with bullies or academic stress. But at college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:38.240
I felt like I could reinvent myself, and become&nbsp;
the kind of person I’d always wanted to be.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.480
As we hopped from building to building along the&nbsp;
tour, checking out the different facilities and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.080
attending a few sample lectures and&nbsp;
seminars, I was only becoming more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.720
confident that college would be the place for&nbsp;
me to finally bloom. It was perfect for me.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.200
But, despite this college being exactly what&nbsp;
I’d wanted in terms of lifestyle and academic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:00.000
pursuits, there was a certain stain on its&nbsp;
reputation. If any university is around long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.360
enough, or any building at all for that matter,&nbsp;
weird incidents are bound to accumulate. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:10.720
places have ghost or cryptid sightings,&nbsp;
this college had...disappearances. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:16.160
not just one or two. Six different students and&nbsp;
one janitor had disappeared from the campus.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.560
The people had nothing in common except for&nbsp;
the fact they frequented the college. Ages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.840
races, genders, and majors varied&nbsp;
across all the missing people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:27.840
and none of them had known each other.&nbsp;
No notes were given, no clues discovered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:34.320
no remains ever found. A number of the cases are&nbsp;
even still open today. It’s a frightening thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:38.560
that people can just drop off the face of&nbsp;
the earth like that and never be found.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.080
There were over half a million&nbsp;
missing person cases in the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:45.120
back in 2020 alone. It’s&nbsp;
more common than you think.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.080
Still, I comforted myself with&nbsp;
the knowledge that thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:51.680
of new students came in and out of the&nbsp;
college every year, and only a handful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:55.840
of disappearances. What were the chances&nbsp;
that something bad would ever happen to me?

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:00.880
Though to be fair, every person who actually&nbsp;
did disappear, in their last moments, thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.000
“What are the chances something&nbsp;
like this would happen to me?” too.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.080
We were several hours into the tour when the&nbsp;
coffee Steve had on the way over finally caught&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:13.120
up with him. He needed to go. Bad. So he asked&nbsp;
our tour guide whether there was a bathroom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:17.280
anywhere around here. She told him that he could&nbsp;
find one pretty easily on the ground floor of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.480
administrative building nearby. In the meantime,&nbsp;
we’d be making our way over to lecture hall 2B,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:25.280
so he could simply catch up&nbsp;
with us there once he was done.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:30.320
Steve left, and I didn’t think anything of it&nbsp;
at the time. I was too wrapped up in the tour.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.160
It was only half an hour later, when we&nbsp;
were finishing up and preparing to leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:39.360
lecture hall 2b, that his absence from the&nbsp;
group suddenly became a lot more noticeable.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:43.920
I started feeling nervous. He’d just gone to&nbsp;
the bathroom, what could be taking so long?

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:48.080
I ran through all the more rational explanations&nbsp;
in my head: Maybe there was a long line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:51.680
maybe the bathroom was out of order when he&nbsp;
got there and he needed to find another one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.000
maybe he got a little lost on the way there,&nbsp;
maybe he got a little lost on the way back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.600
There were so many perfectly rational&nbsp;
reasons he wasn’t with us right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:04.080
But as the minutes dragged on, each one&nbsp;
felt a little less likely, and a dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.800
pit of dread began to form in my stomach. It&nbsp;
wasn’t long before I alerted the tour guide.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.200
Naturally, she tried to calm me down, saying&nbsp;
how likely it was that he’d just gotten lost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:17.680
Steve was a smart kid, he was thinking of going&nbsp;
to this college after all so he must be smart.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:21.520
There was a smattering of laughter from the&nbsp;
tour group. Still, she put the word out to her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.400
fellow staff members and the search began.&nbsp;
Despite trying my best to avoid panicking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.080
the stats about all those disappearances&nbsp;
just kept flashing through my mind.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:33.920
What if we were the unlucky ones? What&nbsp;
if Steve had actually disappeared,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:37.600
just like the others, and I’d never see&nbsp;
him again? As the hours marched on and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:41.840
still wasn’t found, that was all I could&nbsp;
think about. I didn’t want to lose him.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:44.720
I’d tried calling his cell phone&nbsp;
and texting him countless times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:49.040
but got no response. You can only imagine my&nbsp;
relief when I suddenly got a call from him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:52.880
Though when I heard the content of the&nbsp;
call, that relief quickly disappered.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:58.400
The reception was patchy and his voice had a&nbsp;
strange echo to it, like he was somewhere deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:04.560
and cavernous. There was this other noise&nbsp;
in the background, too: This strange, tinny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.920
whine that felt oddly familiar. I only caught&nbsp;
a few words from him before the call cut out.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:16.560
“The door...Stay away...It keeps going...Something&nbsp;
down here...Don’t come looking...Oh god…”

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:20.880
If I was scared before, I was utterly mortified&nbsp;
now. I couldn’t just leave my brother in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:25.760
situation like this, no matter what he told me&nbsp;
to do. I had to find him somehow. I just had to.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:30.480
Nobody else seemed to take it seriously, but I&nbsp;
somehow knew he was in the most terrible danger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.800
That’s when I decided to head to the one place I&nbsp;
believed he would be: The Administrative Building.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:39.200
It was such an unremarkable little building,&nbsp;
hardly the kind of place where you’d expect some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:46.000
terrible secret to be hiding. But dangerous things&nbsp;
often hide in plain sight. Things like...The Door.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.000
It was one of the things from Steve’s&nbsp;
message that had stuck in my head.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:55.440
“The Door.” Call it instinct, call it intuition,&nbsp;
call it blind dumb luck, but when I was standing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:59.360
in front of an average-looking door in&nbsp;
that unremarkable administrative building,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:04.880
I knew I was staring at the exact door that my&nbsp;
brother had been talking about. It radiated bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:09.760
vibes. Dark energy was practically humming&nbsp;
off of it. But I wouldn’t let that stop me.

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:15.200
Perhaps I was stupid, going in without help.&nbsp;
While it’s easy to criticize from the safety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:20.160
of your home, it’s worth remembering: We do silly&nbsp;
things when we’re afraid, and I mean this was my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:24.880
brother we’re talking about here. No one in the&nbsp;
world was more important to me than him. No one.

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:29.680
The second I entered the room beyond the door,&nbsp;
something changed. It was like stepping into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:35.200
another world entirely. The air was thick and&nbsp;
oppressive. The floor below me and the walls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:41.440
around me were ungodly cold. But most remarkable&nbsp;
of all was the dark. Even my phone’s flashlight at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:47.920
full power barely made a dent. I don’t think I’d&nbsp;
ever seen anything so truly, impenetrably dark.

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:51.840
Outside of a certain nightmare, anyway…

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:57.440
That was when I finally realized where I was&nbsp;
standing. At the top of the stairs, those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.120
stairs, the stairs that had been haunting my&nbsp;
restless sleep. A single winding stairwell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.280
down into the void. Every part of me&nbsp;
wanted to run and scream and hide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:10.800
but I couldn’t. I knew that if it were me&nbsp;
trapped down there, Steve wouldn’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:15.360
hesitated. He would have charged down there and&nbsp;
helped me. Now, I had to do the same for him.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:19.520
After all, we all know that in real life,&nbsp;
there are no such things as monsters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:21.920
And how deep could a staircase really go, anyway?

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:26.080
That question was soon put to the test as&nbsp;
I descended down into the pit. I counted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:31.760
thirteen steps each time, before reaching a&nbsp;
small platform. There were strange markings and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:37.680
scratches on the wall, the further I descended.&nbsp;
Every footstep I took echoed on and on and on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:42.320
I must have been walking for a solid&nbsp;
fifteen minutes, traveling impossibly deep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:48.160
when I finally heard something else. I called out&nbsp;
Steve’s name, but quickly realized it wasn’t him.

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:54.080
It was a little girl, weeping, a few hundred&nbsp;
feet below. I couldn’t tell if she was afraid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:59.600
in pain, or both, but hearing it put ice in my&nbsp;
veins. What the hell was going on down there?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:03.760
Had whoever taken her taken Steve,&nbsp;
too? What exactly was I dealing with?

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:07.840
These were the questions racing through my mind&nbsp;
as I got deeper and deeper into the belly of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:13.440
beast. Time ceased to matter as the flights went&nbsp;
on and on beneath me, ever descending into deeper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:18.800
colder darkness. I was literally walking&nbsp;
into my own nightmare, but seeing as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:23.120
only alternative was leaving my brother to&nbsp;
die down here, I couldn’t do anything else.

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:26.160
I was down countless flights of&nbsp;
stairs when I finally saw him.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:30.480
He was a distant, pale spec, dithering&nbsp;
and afraid. He was a full flight down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:34.160
from me. I was so happy to see him that,&nbsp;
at first, I didn’t even notice anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:38.800
was wrong. But as I got closer, I began to&nbsp;
realize that this wasn’t the Steve I knew.&nbsp;

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:43.360
The dread crept into me, spreading through&nbsp;
every cell like a virus. I finally knew why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:48.000
I recognized it: It was the thing from my&nbsp;
dreams. The monster whose appearance always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:52.240
brought those terrible nightmares to an end.&nbsp;
But what did it mean to see it in real life?

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:57.200
It was Steve’s face, but it seemed&nbsp;
larger and deathly pale. I froze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:02.080
and as the face drew closer to me, I realized&nbsp;
it had no irises, no pupils, no nostrils,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:07.200
and no mouth. It wasn’t my brother. It looked&nbsp;
like a terrible, deathlike mask modeled after him.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:11.280
I tried to scream as it appeared right in&nbsp;
front of me, just as I had in all the dreams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:16.480
but it was too late. Long, gnarled fingers&nbsp;
curled around my torso and crushed my ribs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:21.520
It was pain more intense than I thought&nbsp;
possible, and soon, even my vision went black.&nbsp;

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:31.120
“Steve...I’m sorry I couldn’t save&nbsp;
you. I couldn’t even save myself.”

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:35.920
Now go check out “SCP-087 - The&nbsp;
Stairwell” and “The Secret at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:41.840
Bottom of SCP-087 - EXPLAINED” for more takes&nbsp;
on the terrifying secret of the Stairwell!

